The Algorithmic Health Human: Platform Architecture, Epistemic Authority, and the Transformation of Health Belief Formation

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

A Theoretical PhD Dissertation

Field: Human Behavior × Digital Systems × Health Information × AI-Mediated Culture

---

Abstract

The digital transformation of health communication has produced a structural reconfiguration of the human epistemic environment. Medical knowledge historically mediated through physicians, pharmacists, and scientific institutions is now distributed through a decentralized ecosystem in which professionals, influencers, biohackers, commercial actors, patients, and anonymous users compete for attention within algorithmically curated information environments. This dissertation develops a theoretical framework for understanding how this transformation reshapes the acquisition, evaluation, belief formation, and behavioral conversion of health information. The central thesis is that the principal transformation is not the quantity of health information available to individuals, but the architecture through which individuals determine what health information deserves to be believed and acted upon. The dissertation advances four theoretical contributions. First, it distinguishes four irreducible layers—epistemic quality, epistemic authority, persuasive power, and behavioral conversion—which the literature has frequently conflated, and demonstrates that credibility does not reduce to persuasion, nor persuasion to behavior. Second, it introduces the Independence Ratio ($I_D$) as a measurable construct that distinguishes repetition from epistemic diversity, formally capturing the insight that exposure frequency does not equal independent evidential support. Third, it reformulates health belief updating within a Bayesian framework in which source characteristics modulate the weight assigned to evidence rather than the evidence itself, yielding a model in which the same randomized controlled trial is weighted differently depending on the epistemic environment in which it is encountered. Fourth, it introduces Epistemic Visibility ($EV$) as a measurable ratio of informational visibility to independent evidential support, and proposes that health-information risk is a function of Epistemic Visibility misalignment—epistemic underexposure when evidence is strong but visibility is low, and epistemic overexposure when evidence is weak but visibility is high. The dissertation specifies a falsifiable model-comparison architecture, operationalizes a health-equity dimension for cross-cultural contexts, and proposes an intervention framework grounded in prebunking, evidence-quality indicators, and algorithmic diversity nudges. The broader significance concerns a civilizational transition: Homo sapiens now lives inside an environment in which specialized knowledge is instantaneous, abundant, personalized, socially reinforced, and algorithmically filtered. The question is no longer how much health information humanity possesses, but how humanity decides what health information deserves to become belief and behavior.

---

1. Introduction: The Epistemic Transformation

For most of human history, access to specialized health knowledge was constrained by geography, institutions, education, and professional networks. The physician, the pharmacist, the hospital, and the university served as gatekeepers of medical epistemology. Health information was comparatively centralized, and the pathway from individual to diagnosis to intervention was mediated by credentialed authority. This is no longer the case.

The digital environment has dramatically reduced barriers to information access while simultaneously introducing new forms of epistemic mediation. The contemporary individual can construct a personalized model of health from heterogeneous sources: vitamins, supplements, dietary protocols, exercise programs, sleep strategies, laboratory biomarkers, wearable devices, longevity research, pharmaceutical information, anecdotal experiences, and algorithmically recommended content. The same system that democratizes access to high-quality scientific information also amplifies incomplete, exaggerated, commercially motivated, or unsupported health claims.

This dissertation proposes to investigate this phenomenon without assuming that social media is intrinsically harmful or beneficial. The objective is to identify the mechanisms through which digital information environments influence health beliefs, perceived expertise, risk perception, self-experimentation, self-diagnosis, supplement use, and health-related behavior. The central hypothesis is that the principal transformation may not be the quantity of health information available to humans, but the architecture through which humans determine what health information deserves to be believed and acted upon.

A stronger, empirically testable version of this hypothesis posits that the primary risk of the contemporary digital health environment arises from systematic misalignment between evidential value and informational visibility. This dissertation develops the theoretical apparatus to measure that misalignment.

