2. Theoretical Foundations

2.1 From Medical Authority to Distributed Health Authority

Historically, health information was mediated through a comparatively centralized structure:

\text{Individual} \rightarrow \text{Healthcare Professional} \rightarrow \text{Diagnosis} \rightarrow \text{Intervention} 

The contemporary digital environment introduces multiple additional pathways:

\text{Individual} \rightarrow \text{Search Engine} \rightarrow \text{Social Media} \rightarrow \text{Influencer} \rightarrow \text{Community} \rightarrow \text{Algorithm} \rightarrow \text{Supplement/Product} \rightarrow \text{Healthcare Professional} 

or:

\text{Individual} \rightarrow \text{Symptom} \rightarrow \text{Online Search} \rightarrow \text{Self-Diagnosis} \rightarrow \text{Online Community} \rightarrow \text{Self-Treatment} 

Authority can therefore become distributed among actors with radically different levels of expertise. A person's perceived credibility may depend not only on formal qualifications but also on visibility, physical appearance, communication ability, confidence, personal testimony, number of followers, engagement, perceived authenticity, scientific vocabulary, association with prestigious institutions, and commercial presentation.

This produces a foundational distinction:

 \text{Scientific Expertise} 
eq \text{Perceived Expertise} 

and:

 \text{Evidence Quality} 
eq \text{Social Visibility} 

The research must investigate when these variables converge and when they diverge.

2.2 The Four Irreducible Layers

A central theoretical contribution of this dissertation is the explicit separation of four layers that the literature has frequently conflated:

 E 
eq A 
eq P 
eq B 

where:

· $E$ = epistemic quality: the evidential strength of a health claim, evaluated independently of its source.
· $A$ = epistemic authority: who appears qualified to make the claim, as perceived by the individual.
· $P$ = persuasive power: how effectively communication changes attitudes, independent of credibility.
· $B$ = behavioral conversion: whether the individual actually acts upon the belief.

These layers are not linearly related. A source may possess high epistemic authority but low persuasive power. A claim may possess high epistemic quality but low visibility. A communication may be highly persuasive but fail to convert to behavior. The literature on health communication has often treated credibility, persuasion, and behavior as interchangeable outcomes; this dissertation treats them as distinct and potentially divergent.

Empirical support for this separation comes from large-scale randomized experimentation. In a study of young adults, scientists were rated substantially more credible than influencers, yet were not more persuasive on average. Persuasion operated through different mechanisms: scientists' effectiveness rested on perceived reliability and knowledgeability, while influencers' effectiveness rested on personal appeal, prior familiarity, and reputation. Effects persisted for at least six months. This demonstrates empirically that credibility and persuasion can diverge, and that different source types generate different combinations of credibility, persuasion, and behavioral conversion.

The theoretical implication is that the simple binary of "experts versus influencers" is inadequate. The more scientifically interesting question is: which combinations of epistemic quality, authority, persuasion, and behavioral conversion characterize different information sources, and under what conditions does each layer dominate?

