3. The Algorithmic Health Information Environment

3.1 The Human–Algorithm Dynamical System

The contemporary health-information environment is not a static repository of content. It is a coupled human–algorithm dynamical system in which individual behavior generates data, data influences recommendation systems, and recommendation systems modify subsequent information exposure. This produces a feedback loop:

C \rightarrow A \rightarrow P \rightarrow B \rightarrow H \rightarrow T \rightarrow A' 

where:

· $C$ = health-related content
· $A$ = attention
· $P$ = perceived credibility
· $B$ = belief formation
· $H$ = health-related behavior
· $T$ = testimonial or social feedback
· $A'$ = subsequent algorithmic amplification

The critical feature is the feedback loop. The individual does not simply receive information. The individual's behavior also generates data. That data influences recommendation systems. Recommendation systems subsequently modify the individual's information environment. Therefore:

\text{Human Behavior} \rightarrow \text{Algorithmic Adaptation} 

and:

\text{Algorithmic Adaptation} \rightarrow \text{Human Information Exposure} 

This produces a coupled system that cannot be adequately modeled by treating content exposure as an exogenous variable.

3.2 Content as a Multidimensional Vector

Health-related content can be formalized as a vector:

C_j = (E_j, A_j, S_j, M_j, R_j) 

where:

· $E_j$ = evidence quality (tier: randomized controlled trial or meta-analysis, observational study, mechanistic study, anecdotal report)
· $A_j$ = perceived authority (multidimensional: epistemic, relational, aggregate, commercial)
· $S_j$ = social reinforcement (engagement metrics, perceived norms, testimonial density)
· $M_j$ = commercial incentive (disclosure presence, affiliate links, brand mentions)
· $R_j$ = risk disclosure (presence or absence of side effect and interaction warnings)

This vector representation enables systematic content analysis and provides the inputs for the belief-updating model developed in Section 5.

