6. Epistemic Visibility

6.1 Definition

We introduce Epistemic Visibility as a measurable construct that captures the relationship between informational visibility and independent evidential support:

EV_j = \frac{\text{Observed Informational Visibility}_j}{\text{Independent Evidential Support}_j} 

Conceptually, this yields four states:

Evidence Visibility State
High High Aligned
Low Low Aligned
High Low Epistemic Underexposure
Low High Epistemic Overexposure

6.2 Health-Information Risk as Misalignment

The central thesis of this dissertation sharpens to:

\text{Health-Information Risk} \approx f(\text{Epistemic Visibility Misalignment}) 

rather than the simpler formulation:

\text{Social Media} \rightarrow \text{Misinformation} 

Epistemic underexposure occurs when high-quality evidence receives low visibility—for example, a null finding from a large randomized controlled trial that receives minimal engagement. Epistemic overexposure occurs when low-quality evidence receives high visibility—for example, an anecdotal testimonial about a supplement that receives millions of views and is amplified by algorithmic recommendation.

This formulation is falsifiable. If high-evidence, low-visibility claims and low-evidence, high-visibility claims do not differ systematically in their distribution across platforms, the misalignment hypothesis is weakened.

