9. The Optimization–Risk Transition

9.1 From Health Optimization to Health Risk

A particularly important phenomenon is the transition from healthy behavior to excessive optimization. A person may begin with a simple goal—"I want to improve my sleep"—and progressively adopt sleep tracking, supplements, dietary restrictions, fasting, biomarkers, multiple supplements, and increasingly complex protocols. The objective remains health. Yet complexity itself can introduce new risks:

\text{Optimization} \rightarrow \text{Complexity} \rightarrow \text{Interaction} \rightarrow \text{Uncertainty} 

The system may exhibit a non-linear relationship between optimization effort and health benefit:

\text{Health Benefit} = g(\text{Optimization Effort}) - h(\text{Complexity}) - k(\text{Anxiety}) 

where $g(\cdot)$ is a concave benefit function (diminishing returns), $h(\cdot)$ is a convex risk function (interaction effects, adverse events), and $k(\cdot)$ is a psychological burden function (tracking anxiety, orthorexia).

9.2 Clinical Evidence

The optimization-to-risk pathway is clinically documented. Liver injury from herbal and dietary supplements has risen threefold, and non-bodybuilding supplement-induced liver injury led to transplantation in 13% of cases versus 3% for prescription drugs. Biohacking self-experimentation has produced documented adverse events, including intermittent skin infections, high glucose, lipid abnormalities, and heightened resting heart rate.

However, this is not evidence that social media caused those injuries. The causal chain must remain:

\text{Exposure} \rightarrow \text{Belief} \rightarrow \text{Behavior} \rightarrow \text{Health Outcome} 

The research must explicitly prohibit jumping from "supplement harm exists" to "social media causes supplement harm."

9.3 Techno-Hypochondria and Cyberchondria

A distinction must be drawn between cyberchondria (an external search loop, in which individuals repeatedly search for health information online) and techno-hypochondria (an internal biometric loop, in which individuals become dependent on real-time, personalized biometric data streams). Techno-hypochondria is formally defined through three core attributes: biometric data obsession, digital catastrophizing, and pathological need for algorithmic feedback. Antecedents include wearable ownership and low digital health literacy. Consequences include loss of clinical focus, increased risk of medical errors, and weakened professional autonomy.

Techno-hypochondria is a conceptual construct, not yet established evidence that wearable monitoring causes anxiety in the general population. The research must measure false positive rates of device alerts and use scales appropriate to techno-hypochondria rather than relying on cyberchondria scales.

