12. Health Equity Operationalization

The infodemic equity gap framework identifies patterned disparity in exposure to, resilience against, and recoverability from health misinformation. This must be operationalized as a measurable construct, not a paragraph. For the Moroccan context:

Dimension Operationalization
Exposure Language (Darija/Arabic vs. English/French health content)
Resilience Digital health literacy; trust in public health institutions
Recoverability Access to professional care after misinformation exposure

The research should include at least two cultural contexts (MENA and EU/US) to avoid the geographic bias that characterizes most studies on social media and health.

---

13. Intervention Framework

13.1 Prebunking at Scale

A field experiment with $N = 375,597$ Instagram users in the United Kingdom demonstrated that a 19-second prebunking video improved manipulation recognition by 21 percentage points, with effects persisting five months. This provides unusually strong field evidence that a short-form inoculation intervention can produce measurable and durable changes in manipulation recognition at platform scale.

13.2 Evidence-Quality Indicators

Not binary true/false, but uncertainty labels: "preliminary/animal study," "small human trial," "systematic review."

13.3 Algorithmic Diversity Nudge

After three similar health claims, inject content with contrasting evidence or uncertainty framing. This reduces the Convergence Illusion without censorship. Test head-to-head against prebunking in Layer 4 simulation before field trial.

13.4 Health Professional–Influencer Co-Creation

Partner credible scientists with high-reach influencers. This leverages both credibility and persuasion paths, recognizing that scientists and influencers persuade through different mechanisms.

13.5 Digital Health Literacy as Social Determinant

Media health literacy predicts preventive behaviors independently. Digital health literacy contributes additional explanatory power for health self-efficacy.

13.6 Cost-Effectiveness

Prebunking costs approximately $0.05 per user reached for a 21 percentage-point improvement persisting five months. This must be compared against debunking costs in the intervention layer.

